#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hapax-quota-telemetry-writer — live per-provider quota/resource telemetry.

Phase 0.4 (the designed R3 slice) of
REQ-20260609-model-capability-cost-routing-operationalization: the dispatcher's
quota state must come from live telemetry written on a timer, not from the
checked-in ``config/quota-spend-ledger-fixtures.json`` snapshot.

Each tick does four steps:

0. Pull forward the determination producer whose receipts the quota surfaces
   embed (currently the agy admission) whenever the FRESHNESS the next
   publication needs demands it: the pull adds ``--force`` (with
   ``--force-justified-at``, the decision moment) to the same harness exactly
   when the freshest existing admission's ``fresh_until``
   will not cover the next publication deadline — one 600s cycle, slipped by
   the timer's 60s ``AccuracySec``, plus the next tick's bounded pre-write
   work (``FRESHNESS_HORIZON_S``, the sum derived at its definition — cycle +
   accuracy + pre-mint scan + max(lock wait, producer) + anchor→write work;
   the lock wait and the producer timeout are alternatives, never serial,
   because the justified-at instant lets a forced waiter defer, under the
   lock, to a concurrent winner whose run completed after it). Derived from
   the receipt's own horizon,
   never from a measured timer phase — the timer's ``AccuracySec=1min``
   walks the fire phase across the determine cadence, a phase-derived window
   (the first repair's 30s ``--due-within``) leaves a 560s-old admission
   unpulled with 340s of freshness against a 600s cycle (critical finding on
   #4665, round 2), and a 615s horizon still accepted a 620s-remaining
   admission that a +640s slipped fire plus a 27s publish overran — a 47s
   flap (critical finding on #4665, round 3). Without this step at all,
   the receipt read below embeds the PREVIOUS cycle's mint — ~600s of its
   900s TTL already spent — and the #4616 anti-overclaim encoding leaves only
   ~300s of surface freshness per 600s write cadence: a permanent ~50% stale
   duty cycle while the receipt plane itself never lapses (measured
   2026-09-12, REVIEW-ROUTE-FRESHNESS-FLAP-DEFECT-20260912). The run ledger's
   ``ran_at`` aging keeps exactly one run per cadence window regardless of
   which invoker fires first — force shifts WHEN a needed run happens, never
   HOW MANY — and the bounded child budgets (lock wait 90s + producer 90s ≤
   the 210s pull timeout, pinned by TestPullForwardBudgetPins) bound the
   pull child itself; the live-ledger lock wait is likewise an enforced
   bound (``LIVE_LEDGER_LOCK_WAIT_S`` — a bounded wait that expires). The
   pre-mint force-decision scan (``PRE_MINT_SCAN_BOUND_S``) is a MEASURED
   budget, not an execution bound: it runs to completion, its elapsed is
   reported in the summary (``pre_mint_scan_elapsed_s``), and a direct
   overrun predicate degrades the tick's exit code retroactively (rc=4) so
   the overrun is surfaced — never silently truncated, and never claimed as
   pre-empted (codex-1 D1, #4665 round 6). The scan/build/write passes
   (``PRE_WRITE_WORK_BOUND_S``) report their per-pass elapsed in the summary
   (``pre_write_elapsed_s``, ``pre_write_rebuild_elapsed_s`` — claude-1
   minor, #4665 round 12) but still have no direct overrun predicate: their
   budget is enforced indirectly, through the
   freshness-at-publication and continuity witnesses below, which charge
   every pre-publication second against the admissions' remaining lifetime
   — a pass that overruns its bound but publishes with horizon-remaining
   freshness still exits 0 (codex-1 D2, #4665 round 8; the budget bounds the
   horizon derivation, not the exit code by itself).

1. Rebuild and write the live ledger (step 3's scan/build, below) BEFORE any
   receipt refresh, so downstream consumers of the ledger always see the
   freshest embedded evidence first.

2. Refresh the per-provider platform capability receipts (quota / resource /
   capability surfaces) via
   ``scripts/hapax-platform-capability-receipts --all --codex-exec-auth-probe``.
   This MUST run after the step-1 write: the refresh validates subscription
   routes against the live ledger ON DISK and caps each receipt's stale_after
   by that ledger's fresh_until — refreshing against the previous cycle's
   ledger clamps a fresh 900s admission to the stale ledger's remaining
   freshness (critical finding on #4665). When the refreshed receipts change
   the Codex auth blocker, the ledger is rebuilt and rewritten once more so
   the published surface reflects the post-refresh Codex state in the same
   tick. The registry freshness check reads these receipts; the quota surface
   carries ``stale_after=15m``, so the paired systemd timer fires inside that
   window to keep subscription routes from HOLDing on
   ``quota_telemetry_stale_or_unknown``.

3. Rebuild the live quota/spend ledger at
   ``~/.cache/hapax/orchestration/quota-spend-ledger-live.json`` (or
   ``$HAPAX_QUOTA_SPEND_LEDGER_LIVE``). Governance records (transition budgets,
   dependencies, provenance, renewals) are carried over from the checked-in
   base unchanged. Spend receipts are carried over from the base plus validated
   GLMCP PAYG spend receipts reserved by ``scripts/hapax-glmcp-reviewer`` before
   a paid fallback call; telemetry never grants spend authority. The
   telemetry fields are re-derived live:

   - ``captured_at``: now.
   - subscription ``quota_snapshots``: per-platform state from the quota-wall
     relay receipts (``~/.cache/hapax/relay/receipts/<role>-quota-wall.yaml``,
     written by ``shared.quota_wall`` on observed 429/quota-exhaustion). An
     unexpired wall marks the platform ``exhausted`` on the subscription-wall
     path; Claude and GLMCP routes are ``unknown`` unless a fresh, short-lived
     route-specific admission receipt exists. Agy reviewer admission remains
     bounded to its sanctioned smoke receipt path. Other established subscription
     platforms remain wall-absence fresh until their route policy requires a
     receipt-bounded admission plane.
     Coding Plan quota walls block Coding Plan receipts, but a fresh
     endpoint-matched PAYG admission can supersede that subscription wall only
     when it is bound to the active Coding Plan quota-wall witness and a
     validated PAYG spend receipt reservation.
     Recheck admission evidence with
     ``find ~/.cache/hapax/relay/receipts -maxdepth 1 -name '*glmcp-quota-admission*.yaml' -print``
     and ``scripts/hapax-quota-telemetry-writer --json``.
     If a receipt exists but GLMCP remains ``unknown``, read this writer's
     stderr warnings; malformed, stale, mismatched, or unbounded receipts are
     ignored loudly.
     Recheck Claude and agy reviewer admission evidence with
     ``find ~/.cache/hapax/relay/receipts -maxdepth 1 -name '*claude-subscription-quota-admission*.yaml' -print``
     and
     ``find ~/.cache/hapax/relay/receipts -maxdepth 1 -name '*agy-quota-admission*.yaml' -print``
     and create fresh short-lived Claude admission receipts only after a genuine
     account-live subscription observation, or agy admission receipts only after
     a sanctioned ``scripts/hapax-agy-reviewer`` smoke succeeds.
     Admission receipts with ``stale_after_seconds`` above 3600 seconds are
     intentionally ignored; write a fresh short-lived witness instead.
     The emergency false-negative recovery path is to create a fresh
     short-lived admission receipt. Claude receipts must include schema, status,
     provider, capacity_pool, route_id, auth_surface, observation, observed_at,
     stale_after_seconds, evidence_ref, secret_source, secret_value_persisted,
     prompt_or_output_persisted, billing_mode, account_live_quota_observed,
     lane_presence_used_as_quota_evidence, and positive_admission. GLMCP/agy
     receipts additionally bind the reviewer tool/model/endpoint or smoke
     witness fields required by their scanners. Then rerun this writer so the
     live ledger is refreshed.
     That receipt-plane refresh is the canonical emergency procedure for a
     false negative.
     There is intentionally no environment-variable bypass for stale/unknown
     subscription quota. ``evidence_ref`` values are limited to 3-240 safe
     witness-reference characters.
   - ``spend_receipts``: base governance receipts plus GLMCP PAYG estimated
     spend receipts from ``*glmcp-payg-spend*.yaml``. These receipts are
     validated through the inert ``SpendReceipt`` model before they can reduce
     remaining paid-spend caps.
   - ``local_resource_state`` + local-compute snapshot: nvidia-smi VRAM
     pressure and load average, fail-closed to ``unknown`` when unprobeable.

The output is validated through ``QuotaSpendLedger.model_validate`` before an
atomic replace, so a bad tick can never clobber a good live ledger with junk.
No secrets are persisted. Capability receipt refresh performs bounded live route
probes where the route requires them: GLMCP invokes
``hapax-glmcp-reviewer --check`` and Codex invokes a saved-login ``codex exec``
sentinel on the effective dispatch host with inherited Codex auth env stripped. Both
record redacted availability metadata only; local resource probes remain local
files and nvidia-smi. If the Codex sentinel reports an invalidated saved login,
the dispatch host login must be repaired through the documented operator
credential-transfer fallback and then rechecked; telemetry never mints, copies,
or refreshes Codex bearer material itself.

Recheck (the load-bearing ordering and freshness claims above, so a future
reader never has to rediscover them):
``tests/scripts/test_hapax_quota_telemetry_writer.py::test_main_pulls_mints_writes_then_refreshes``
proves the write→refresh ordering and the freshness-derived force predicate
against stubbed producers; ``TestPullForwardBudgetPins`` pins the child budget
arithmetic to the systemd unit files; live:
``journalctl --user -u hapax-quota-telemetry.service --since -1h | grep -E 'DEGRADED|pull-forward'``
plus the ``admission_freshness_at_publication_s`` / ``pull_forward`` keys in this
writer's ``--json`` summary are the operating truth.
"""

